solution:
Altogether the school is $8+7=15$ miles west and $8$ miles north of Kevin's home. The straight path is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $15$ and $8$:
\[
\sqrt{15^2+8^2}=\sqrt{289}=17 \text{ miles}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair H=(0,0),P1=(-8,0),P2=(-8,8),Sc=(-15,8);
draw(H--P1--P2--Sc,linewidth(0.9));
draw(H--Sc,dashed+blue);
draw(P1--P1+(0.8,0)--P1+(0.8,0.8)--P1+(0,0.8));
dot(H);dot(Sc);
label("home",H,SE);label("school",Sc,NW);
label("$8$",(H+P1)/2,S);label("$8$",(-8,6.6),W);label("$7$",(P2+Sc)/2,N);
draw((-15,0)--P1,dotted);draw((-15,0)--Sc,dotted);
label("$15$",((-15,0)+P1)/2,S);
label("$17$",H+0.25*(Sc-H),NE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The bus route is $8+8+7=23$ miles, so the direct trip would be $23-17=\boxed{6}$ miles shorter.

From each of the $17$ vertices, diagonals go to every vertex except itself and its two neighbours, that is to $14$ vertices. This gives $17\cdot14$ diagonal ends, and each diagonal has two ends, so the number of diagonals is
\[
\dfrac{17\cdot14}{2}=\boxed{119}.
\]
(The number $14$ is the number of diagonals from a single vertex, not the total; the figure shows these $14$ diagonals from one vertex $V$ of a $17$-gon.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;
for(int i=0;i<17;++i) P.push(dir(90+360*i/17));
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--P[6]--P[7]--P[8]--P[9]--P[10]--P[11]--P[12]--P[13]--P[14]--P[15]--P[16]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=2;i<16;++i) draw(P[0]--P[i],blue+linewidth(0.5));
for(int i=0;i<17;++i) dot(P[i],linewidth(3));
label("$V$",P[0],N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Here $s=\dfrac{a+b+(a+b)}{2}=a+b=c$, so $s-c=0$ and
\[
[ABC]=\sqrt{(a+b)\cdot b\cdot a\cdot0}=\boxed{0}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),C=(3,0),B=(7,0);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$C$",C,S);label("$B$",B,E);
draw((0,0.45)--(3,0.45),Arrows(4));label("$b$",(1.5,0.45),N);
draw((3,0.45)--(7,0.45),Arrows(4));label("$a$",(5,0.45),N);
draw((0,-0.75)--(7,-0.75),Arrows(4));label("$a+b$",(3.5,-0.75),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
