With base $AB$, the third vertex $C$ lies on line $AB$, so the height is $0$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot c\cdot0=0$ as well. So the area of a degenerate triangle is defined and equals $0$: the three points enclose no region. (Heron's formula also shows the converse: for positive sides, the area is $0$ exactly when one side equals the sum of the other two.)

The segments $AB$ and $CD$ join two disjoint pairs of vertices. Only the unordered pair $\bigl\{\{A,B\},\{C,D\}\bigr\}$ of vertex pairs matters, and by symmetry it is equally likely to be any of the
\[
\dfrac{\binom82\binom62}{2}=\dfrac{28\cdot15}{2}=210
\]
unordered pairs of disjoint vertex pairs.

Two segments joining four distinct vertices can meet only at an interior point of both, so the four vertices must be coplanar and the segments must be the two diagonals of the quadrilateral they form. Sets of four coplanar vertices of a cube are the $6$ faces and the $6$ ``diagonal rectangles'' through two opposite edges. In each of these $12$ rectangles exactly one pair of segments crosses: the two diagonals (the two sides of a pair of opposite sides are parallel and never meet). In the figure, the red pair is the diagonals of a face (the right face) and the blue pair is two space diagonals, which are the diagonals of a diagonal rectangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.5*z,y+0.35*z);}
pair A0=P(0,0,0),B0=P(1,0,0),C0=P(1,1,0),D0=P(0,1,0),A1=P(0,0,1),B1=P(1,0,1),C1=P(1,1,1),D1=P(0,1,1);
draw(A0--B0--C0--D0--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B0--B1--C1--C0,linewidth(0.8));draw(D0--D1--C1,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A0--A1,dashed);draw(A1--B1,dashed);draw(A1--D1,dashed);
draw(B0--C1,red+linewidth(0.8));draw(C0--B1,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A0--C1,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(D0--B1,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(A0);dot(B0);dot(C0);dot(D0);dot(A1);dot(B1);dot(C1);dot(D1);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the probability is $\dfrac{12}{210}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{35}}$.

question:
A figure consists of a unit square with four more unit squares attached, one to each of its sides (a plus sign made of five squares). In each of the five squares both diagonals are drawn, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] sq={(0,0),(1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)};
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){pair p=sq[i];
 draw(p--p+(1,0)--p+(1,1)--p+(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
 draw(p--p+(1,1),linewidth(0.6));draw(p+(1,0)--p+(0,1),linewidth(0.6));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

How many triangles of any size are in the figure?

There are $1000$ people in line for a game. On a table are $1000$ cups face down; exactly one cup has $1000$ dollars under it and the others have nothing. The people come up one at a time; each chooses one of the remaining cups uniformly at random. If the cup has the money, that person takes the $1000$ dollars and the game ends (nobody behind plays). If not, the person takes that empty cup away and the next person plays with the remaining cups.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A claim says that one position $n$ in line has the highest expected payoff. Show that this is false: compute the expected payoff of the person in position $n$.
\item[(b)] Now everybody who actually plays must first pay a fee of $c$ dollars, where $0<c<1000$. Which position in line has the highest expected payoff, and what is it?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the circles have centres $O_1$ (radius $c$) and $O_2$ (radius $r$) and meet at $P$ and $Q$, so $PQ=a$. The line $O_1O_2$ is the perpendicular bisector of $PQ$; let it meet $PQ$ at its midpoint $M$. In right triangle $O_1MP$,
\[
O_1M=\sqrt{c^2-\dfrac{a^2}{4}}=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{4c^2-a^2}=:d .
\]
Since $d<c<b=O_1O_2$, the centre $O_2$ cannot lie between $M$ and $O_1$. So there are two cases.

\textbf{Centres on opposite sides of the chord} (the usual picture): $O_2M=b-d$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(-4,0),O2=(3,0),Pp=(0,3),Q=(0,-3);
draw(circle(O1,5),linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(O2,sqrt(18)),linewidth(0.8));
draw(Pp--Q,red+linewidth(0.8));draw(O1--O2,dashed);draw(O1--Pp,blue);draw(O2--Pp,deepgreen);
draw((0,0)--(0.4,0)--(0.4,0.4)--(0,0.4));
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(Pp);dot(Q);
label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);label("$P$",Pp,2.5*N);label("$Q$",Q,2.5*S);
label("$c$",(O1+Pp)/2,NW,blue);label("$r$",(O2+Pp)/2,NE,deepgreen);
label("$a$",(0,-0.9),E,red);label("$b$",(-2.6,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Centres on the same side}, with $O_1$ between $M$ and $O_2$: $O_2M=b+d$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(-4,0),O2=(-11,0),Pp=(0,3),Q=(0,-3);
draw(circle(O2,sqrt(130)),linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(O1,5),linewidth(0.8));
draw(Pp--Q,red+linewidth(0.8));draw(O2--(0,0),dashed);draw(O1--Pp,blue);draw(O2--Pp,deepgreen);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(Pp);dot(Q);
label("$O_1$",O1,S);label("$O_2$",O2,S);label("$P$",Pp,NE);label("$Q$",Q,SE);
label("$c$",(O1+Pp)/2,SE,blue);label("$r$",(O2+Pp)/2,N,deepgreen);
label("$a$",(0,-1.4),W,red);label("$b$",(O1+O2)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In right triangle $O_2MP$, $r^2=\dfrac{a^2}{4}+O_2M^2=\dfrac{a^2}{4}+b^2\mp2bd+d^2=b^2+c^2\mp2bd$. Hence
\[
\boxed{r=\sqrt{b^2+c^2-b\sqrt{4c^2-a^2}}\quad\text{or}\quad r=\sqrt{b^2+c^2+b\sqrt{4c^2-a^2}}},
\]
the first when the centres are on opposite sides of the common chord and the second when they are on the same side. (The figures use $a=6$, $c=5$, $b=7$, giving $r=\sqrt{18}$ and $r=\sqrt{130}$.)

Every interior angle of an equiangular hexagon is $120^\circ$. Extend sides $AB$, $CD$ and $EF$ until they meet at $X$ (lines $AB$, $CD$), $Y$ (lines $CD$, $EF$) and $Z$ (lines $EF$, $AB$). The triangles $BXC$, $DYE$ and $FZA$ have two angles of $180^\circ-120^\circ=60^\circ$, so they are equilateral, with sides $BC=2$, $DE=1$ and $FA$. Then triangle $XYZ$ has all angles $60^\circ$, so it is equilateral.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=A+4*dir(0),C=B+2*dir(60),D=C+7*dir(120),Ep=D+1*dir(180),F=Ep+5*dir(240);
pair X=extension(A,B,C,D),Y=extension(C,D,Ep,F),Z=extension(Ep,F,A,B);
draw(B--X--C,dashed+gray);draw(D--Y--Ep,dashed+gray);draw(F--Z--A,dashed+gray);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ep--F--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$A$",A,dir(240));label("$B$",B,dir(300));label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,dir(60));label("$E$",Ep,dir(120));label("$F$",F,W);
label("$4$",(A+B)/2,N);label("$2$",(B+C)/2,W);label("$7$",(C+D)/2,SW);label("$1$",(D+Ep)/2,S);
label("$5$",(Ep+F)/2,E);label("$4$",(F+A)/2,NE);
label("$X$",X,SE);label("$Y$",Y,N);label("$Z$",Z,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its sides are
\[
ZX=FA+AB+BC=FA+6,\qquad XY=BC+CD+DE=10,\qquad YZ=DE+EF+FA=1+EF+FA .
\]
From $ZX=XY$, $FA=4$; from $YZ=XY$, $1+EF+4=10$, so $EF=5$. Hence $\boxed{EF=5,\ FA=4}$.

question:
A contest asked for the $12$th term of the sequence $2,9,28,\dots$ with choices $37$, $65$, $1728$, $1729$, and the key gave $1729$ (the rule $n^3+1$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the rule $a_n=n^3+1$ fits the given terms and gives $a_{12}=1729$.
\item[(b)] Show that the given terms do not determine $a_{12}$: for every real number $v$ there is a polynomial $p$ of degree at most $3$ with $p(1)=2$, $p(2)=9$, $p(3)=28$ and $p(12)=v$. Find such a polynomial with $p(12)=37$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write a positive integer as $N=10x+e$, where $e$ is its last digit and $x$ is the number formed by the other digits. Prove that $17\mid N$ if and only if $17\mid x-5e$. (So the test can be repeated on smaller and smaller numbers.)
\item[(b)] Generalise: for any positive integer $m$ with $\gcd(m,10)=1$, give a similar rule ``$m\mid N$ if and only if $m\mid x-te$'' and prove it.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
``Indistinguishable'' means that the two T's are identical, so collections that differ only in which T was taken are the same collection; we count collections by which letters they contain, not by which magnets.

The vowels are A, O, U (all different): $\binom32=3$ choices of two.

The consonants are M, T, H, C, N, T, S: six different letters, with T appearing twice. Choose three by the number of T's:
\begin{itemize}
\item no T: $\binom53=10$ (from M, H, C, N, S);
\item one T: $\binom52=10$;
\item two T's: $\binom51=5$.
\end{itemize}
That gives $25$ consonant collections. In total there are $3\cdot25=\boxed{75}$ collections.

By the shoelace formula the area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\left|x(3x-5x)+18(5x-2)+6(2-3x)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|-2x^2+72x-24\right|=\left|-x^2+36x-12\right| .
\]
The polynomials whose absolute value equals this for all $x$ are $\pm\left(-x^2+36x-12\right)$; the one with $p(0)<0$ is $p(x)=-x^2+36x-12$. The sum of its coefficients is $p(1)=-1+36-12=\boxed{23}$. For $x=1$ the triangle is the one shown, with area $23$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P1=(1,2),P2=(18,3),P3=(6,5);
draw((-0.5,0)--(19.5,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,6.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(19.5,0),SE);label("$y$",(0,6.5),NW);
draw(P1--P2--P3--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(P3);
label("$(1,2)$",P1,SE);label("$(18,3)$",P2,SE);label("$(6,5)$",P3,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Numbers $r,s,t$ are the roots (with multiplicity) of the monic cubic $(x-r)(x-s)(x-t)=x^3+ax^2+bx+c$ with $a=-(r+s+t)$, $b=rs+st+tr$, $c=-rst$; $b$ is unrestricted. So we need
\[
r+s+t\in\{-1,0\}\quad\text{and}\quad rst\in\{-1,0\}.
\]
If none of the numbers is $0$, they are $\pm1$; then $rst=-1$ needs one or three $-1$'s, with sums $1$ or $-3$, which fail. So at least one number is $0$ (and then $rst=0$), and we need the sum to be $-1$ or $0$. Count ordered triples:
\begin{itemize}
\item sum $0$: $(0,0,0)$, and the arrangements of $\{0,1,-1\}$: $1+6=7$;
\item sum $-1$: arrangements of $\{0,0,-1\}$: $3$.
\end{itemize}
That is $10$ of the $27$ equally likely triples, so the probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{10}{27}}$.

After the corners are cut out, the base is $(x-4)\times(y-4)$ and the folded sides have height $2$ (dashed lines are the folds).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=13,y=16;
filldraw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle,lightgray,gray);
filldraw((x-2,0)--(x,0)--(x,2)--(x-2,2)--cycle,lightgray,gray);
filldraw((0,y-2)--(2,y-2)--(2,y)--(0,y)--cycle,lightgray,gray);
filldraw((x-2,y-2)--(x,y-2)--(x,y)--(x-2,y)--cycle,lightgray,gray);
draw((0,0)--(x,0)--(x,y)--(0,y)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((2,2)--(x-2,2)--(x-2,y-2)--(2,y-2)--cycle,dashed);
label("$x$",(x/2,0),S);label("$y$",(x,y/2),E);
label("$2$",(1,2),N);label("$2$",(2,1),E);
label("$x-4$",(x/2,2),N);label("$y-4$",(2,y/2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the volume is $2(x-4)(y-4)=216$, i.e.\ $(x-4)(y-4)=108$. With $u=x-4$ and $v=y-4$ positive integers, the ordered pairs correspond to the ordered factorisations $uv=108=2^2\cdot3^3$, one for each divisor $u$ of $108$. The number of divisors is $(2+1)(3+1)=12$, so there are $\boxed{12}$ ordered pairs.

question:
Let $p$, $q$ and $r$ be the roots of $x^3+20x^2+18x-2018$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Compute $\dfrac{pqr}{2(pq+qr+rp)}$, which would be the ratio of the volume to the surface area of a rectangular prism with edge lengths $p,q,r$.
\item[(b)] Show that, in fact, no rectangular prism has edge lengths $p$, $q$, $r$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $x$ is a root of $x^2+k$, $k=-x^2=-(a+b-2c)^2$, so we want $|a+b-2c|=(c-a)+(c-b)$ as small as possible. As the triangle is scalene with $c$ largest, $c-a$ and $c-b$ are different positive integers, so their sum is at least $1+2=3$, and the sum $3$ means the sides are $c-2,c-1,c$: consecutive, which is excluded. The sum cannot be $3$ otherwise, so it is at least $4$, attained by $\{c-a,c-b\}=\{1,3\}$, e.g.\ sides $2,4,5$ (a valid triangle as $2+4>5$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=4,bb=2,c=5;
real cx=(bb*bb+c*c-a*a)/(2*c);
pair A=(0,0),B=(c,0),C=(cx,sqrt(bb*bb-cx*cx));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$2$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$4$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $x=-4$ and the largest $k$ is $-16$. The answer is $\boxed{-16}$.

question:
A parabola is the set of points equidistant from a fixed point (the focus) and a fixed line (the directrix) not through the focus. Its axis need not be vertical or horizontal.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find an equation of the parabola with focus $F=(1,1)$ and directrix $x+y=0$.
\item[(b)] Prove that every parabola has an equation of the form $Ax^2+Bxy+Cy^2+Dx+Ey+G=0$ with $A,B,C$ not all zero and $B^2=4AC$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The distance from $(x,y)$ to the line $x+y=0$ is $\dfrac{|x+y|}{\sqrt2}$. Equating squared distances,
\[
(x-1)^2+(y-1)^2=\dfrac{(x+y)^2}{2}\iff2x^2-4x+2+2y^2-4y+2=x^2+2xy+y^2,
\]
so the parabola is $\boxed{x^2-2xy+y^2-4x-4y+4=0}$. Its axis is the line $y=x$ and its vertex is $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, halfway between $F$ and the foot $(0,0)$ of the perpendicular from $F$ to the directrix.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f=sqrt(2)/2;
pair V=(0.5,0.5),u=dir(45),w=dir(135);
pair g(real t){return V+(t^2/(4*f))*u+t*w;}
draw((-3.2,0)--(4.2,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-3.2)--(0,4.2),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4.2,0),SE);label("$y$",(0,4.2),NW);
draw((-2.8,2.8)--(2.8,-2.8),dashed+blue);
draw(graph(g,-3.1,3.1),linewidth(0.9)+red);
pair F=(1,1);dot(F);label("$F(1,1)$",F,SE);
label("$x+y=0$",(2.3,-2.3),NE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Write the directrix as $ux+vy+w=0$ with $u^2+v^2=1$ (divide any equation of the line by $\sqrt{u^2+v^2}$), and let the focus be $(p,q)$. The distance from $(x,y)$ to the line is $|ux+vy+w|$, so the parabola is
\[
(x-p)^2+(y-q)^2=(ux+vy+w)^2 .
\]
Its quadratic part is
\[
x^2+y^2-(ux+vy)^2=\left(1-u^2\right)x^2-2uv\,xy+\left(1-v^2\right)y^2=v^2x^2-2uv\,xy+u^2y^2=(vx-uy)^2 .
\]
So $A=v^2$, $B=-2uv$, $C=u^2$ (not all zero since $u^2+v^2=1$), and $B^2-4AC=4u^2v^2-4u^2v^2=0$. $\blacksquare$

In (a), $A=1$, $B=-2$, $C=1$ and indeed $B^2=4AC$. The equation of a given parabola is unique only up to multiplying it by a nonzero constant; a tilted parabola has an $xy$ term, and rotating the axes by the angle of its axis removes that term and gives the familiar form $Y=kX^2$.

Write $n=7q+r$ with $0\le r\le6$. If $r=0$, both sides equal $q$. If $r\ge1$, then $\left\lceil\dfrac {n}{7}\right\rceil=q+1$, while $\left[\dfrac {n}{7}\right]=q+1$ exactly when $\dfrac {r}{7}>\dfrac{1}{2}$, i.e.\ $r\in\{4,5,6\}$ (no tie occurs since $\dfrac {r}{7}\ne\dfrac{1}{2}$). So we count $1\le n\le99$ with $n\equiv0,4,5,6\pmod7$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $r=0$: $7,14,\dots,98$: $14$ numbers;
\item $r=4$: $4,11,\dots,95$: $14$; $r=5$: $5,\dots,96$: $14$; $r=6$: $6,\dots,97$: $14$.
\end{itemize}
The answer is $14\cdot4=\boxed{56}$.

question:
Seven different digits are chosen from $0,1,2,\dots,9$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many ordered arrangements (rows of seven different digits) are possible?
\item[(b)] How many different groups of seven digits (order not mattering) are possible?
\end{enumerate}

Two semicircles with centres $P$ and $Q$ and radii $6$ and $4$ stand on the diameter of a large semicircle. They are tangent to each other and to the large semicircle. A line is tangent to semicircle $P$ at $L$ and to semicircle $Q$ at $M$, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(-4,0),Q=(6,0);
pair nn=(1,sqrt(24))/5;
pair L=P+6*nn,M=Q+4*nn;
draw(arc(O,10,0,180),linewidth(0.9));draw(arc(P,6,0,180),linewidth(0.8));draw(arc(Q,4,0,180),linewidth(0.8));
draw((-10,0)--(10,0),linewidth(0.9));
draw(L+0.25*(L-M)--M+0.4*(M-L),linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(L);dot(M);
label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$L$",L,N);label("$M$",M,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Find $LM$.

solution:
The two small semicircles touch each other, so $PQ=6+4=10$ (and they fit exactly in a large semicircle of radius $10$). The radii $PL$ and $QM$ are perpendicular to the tangent line $LM$, so they are parallel. Let $H$ be the point on $PL$ with $PH=QM=4$; then $HLMQ$ is a rectangle, so $HQ=LM$, $HL=6-4=2$, and triangle $PHQ$ has a right angle at $H$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(-4,0),Q=(6,0);
pair nn=(1,sqrt(24))/5;
pair L=P+6*nn,M=Q+4*nn,H=P+4*nn;
draw(arc(O,10,0,180),gray);draw(arc(P,6,0,180),linewidth(0.8));draw(arc(Q,4,0,180),linewidth(0.8));
draw((-10,0)--(10,0),linewidth(0.9));
draw(L--M,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--L,blue);draw(Q--M,blue);draw(H--Q,dashed+red);
draw(M+0.5*unit(L-M)--M+0.5*unit(L-M)+0.5*unit(P-L)--M+0.5*unit(P-L));
draw(L+0.5*unit(M-L)--L+0.5*unit(M-L)+0.5*unit(P-L)--L+0.5*unit(P-L));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(L);dot(M);dot(H);
label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$L$",L,N);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$H$",H,W);
label("$4$",(Q+M)/2,E,blue);label("$4$",(P+H)/2,W,blue);label("$2$",(H+L)/2,W,blue);
draw((-4,-1.7)--(6,-1.7),Arrows(4));label("$10$",(1,-1.7),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem,
\[
LM=HQ=\sqrt{PQ^2-PH^2}=\sqrt{10^2-2^2}=\sqrt{96}=\boxed{4\sqrt6}.
\]

Use vectors with origin $B$, writing $\vec A$ and $\vec C$ for the position vectors of $A$ and $C$. Then
\[
\vec E=\dfrac{1}{4}\vec A,\qquad\vec D=\dfrac{4}{5}\vec C,\qquad\vec F=\dfrac{1}{6}\vec E+\dfrac{5}{6}\vec D=\dfrac{1}{24}\vec A+\dfrac{2}{3}\vec C .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(1.8,7),C=(7,0);
pair Ep=(3*B+A)/4,D=(4*C+B)/5,F=(Ep+5*D)/6,G=extension(B,F,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ep--D,linewidth(0.8));draw(B--G,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(Ep);dot(D);dot(F);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$D$",D,S);label("$F$",F,dir(80));label("$G$",G,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A point $t\vec F$ on line $BF$ lies on line $AC$ exactly when its coefficients of $\vec A$ and $\vec C$ add up to $1$:
\[
t\left(\dfrac{1}{24}+\dfrac{16}{24}\right)=1\iff t=\dfrac{24}{17}.
\]
So $\vec G=\dfrac{24}{17}\vec F=\dfrac{1}{17}\vec A+\dfrac{16}{17}\vec C$ (so $AG:GC=16:1$), and $BG=\dfrac{24}{17}BF$. Hence $FG=\dfrac{7}{17}BF$ and
\[
BF:FG=\boxed{17:7}.
\]
The problem is consistent. The poster's mass points ($A=1$, $B=3$, $C=12$) balance $E$ and $D$, and they locate the point where $ED$ meets the cevian from $B$ to the point $G_0$ with $AG_0:G_0C=12:1$, giving $15:4$ on $ED$. That point is not $F$ (since $15:4\ne5:1$), so $G\ne G_0$ and those masses do not apply to line $BG$.

Since $AB=AC$, the base angles are equal: $\angle ACB=\angle ABC=70^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(4,0),A=(2,2*Tan(70));
pair Ep=extension(C,C+dir(145),B,A);
pair D=extension(Ep,Ep+(1,0),A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--Ep,linewidth(0.8));draw(Ep--D,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(C,0.8,145,180),linewidth(0.6));draw(arc(C,0.95,110,145),linewidth(0.6));
draw(arc(Ep,0.9,-35,0),linewidth(0.6));
label("$35^\circ$",C+1.3*dir(164),fontsize(8pt));
label("$35^\circ$",C+1.55*dir(128),fontsize(8pt));
label("$35^\circ$",Ep+1.5*dir(-17.5),fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(B,0.6,0,70),linewidth(0.6));label("$70^\circ$",B+1.05*dir(35),fontsize(8pt));
path tk(pair P,pair Q){pair M=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P)*0.18,v=rotate(90)*u;return (M-v)--(M+v);}
draw(tk(C,D));draw(tk(Ep,D));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$D$",D,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

As $E$ lies on side $AB$, the ray $CE$ lies inside $\angle ACB$, so
\[
\angle DCE=\angle ACE=\angle ACB-\angle ECB=70^\circ-35^\circ=35^\circ .
\]
Triangle $CDE$ is isosceles with $CD=DE$, so its base angles at $C$ and $E$ are equal: $\angle DEC=\angle DCE=35^\circ$.

Hence $\angle DEC=\angle ECB=35^\circ$. These are alternate angles formed by the transversal $EC$ with the lines $DE$ and $BC$ ($D$ and $B$ lie on opposite sides of line $CE$). Equal alternate angles give $DE\parallel BC$. $\blacksquare$

(So $CE$ bisects $\angle ACB$, and the result is the general fact that the bisector from $C$ makes $CD=DE$ exactly when $DE\parallel BC$.)

Compare $5^{n+1}=5\cdot5^n$ with $5^n$. Write $5^n=m\cdot10^k$ with $1\le m<10$ (so $5^n$ has $k+1$ digits and its leading digit is $\lfloor m\rfloor$).
\begin{itemize}
\item If the leading digit is $1$, then $1\le m<2$ and $5\le5m<10$: $5^{n+1}$ has the \emph{same} number of digits.
\item If the leading digit is at least $2$, then $10\le5m<50$: $5^{n+1}$ has exactly \emph{one more} digit.
\end{itemize}
Going from $5^0=1$ ($1$ digit) to $5^{2018}$ ($1411$ digits) takes $2018$ steps, and the number of digits grows by $1410$. So exactly $1410$ of the steps $n=0,1,\dots,2017$ add a digit, and the other $2018-1410=608$ start from a power with leading digit $1$. One of these is $5^0=1$, so among $n=1,\dots,2017$ there are $607$ powers with leading digit $1$. Finally $5^{2018}$ has leading digit $3$. The answer is $\boxed{607}$.

question:
A function $f$ is its own inverse if $f(f(x))=x$ for every $x$ in its domain.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that a function can be its own inverse by checking that $f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x}$ (for $x\ne0$) and $g(x)=5-x$ are.
\item[(b)] Find all real numbers $a$ and $b$ for which the linear function $h(x)=ax+b$ (defined for all real $x$) is its own inverse.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The squares up to $100$ are $0,1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100$. Listing the sums $a^2+b^2\le100$ with $0\le a\le b$ by the larger square $b^2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $b\le3$: $1,2,4,5,8,9,10,13,18$;
\item $b=4$: $16,17,20,25,32$;
\item $b=5$: $25,26,29,34,41,50$;
\item $b=6$: $36,37,40,45,52,61,72$;
\item $b=7$: $49,50,53,58,65,74,85,98$;
\item $b=8$: $64,65,68,73,80,89,100$;
\item $b=9$: $81,82,85,90,97$;
\item $b=10$: $100$.
\end{itemize}
Removing repeats ($25$, $50$, $65$, $85$, $100$ each appear twice), the distinct values are
\[
1,2,4,5,8,9,10,13,16,17,18,20,25,26,29,32,34,36,37,40,41,45,49,50,52,53,58,61,64,65,68,72,73,74,80,81,82,85,89,90,97,98,100,
\]
which is $\boxed{43}$ numbers.

(This agrees with the two-square theorem: $n$ is a sum of two squares exactly when every prime $p\equiv3\pmod4$ divides $n$ to an even power. If $0$ is not allowed, so that both squares are positive, the answer is $35$: the eight perfect squares $1,4,9,16,36,49,64,81$ that are not also sums of two positive squares drop out.)

For a point $(x,y)$ on $y^2=4ax$, the distance to the focus $(a,0)$ equals the distance to the directrix $x=-a$, which is $x+a$ (as $x\ge0$). So the condition is $x+a=k$, i.e.\ $a=k-x$, which requires $0\le x<k$ (as $a>0$).

Eliminate the parameter: $y^2=4ax=4(k-x)x$, that is,
\[
\boxed{y^2=4kx-4x^2}\qquad\text{or}\qquad \dfrac{\left(x-\dfrac {k}{2}\right)^2}{\left(\dfrac {k}{2}\right)^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{k^2}=1 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real k=4;
pair Fa=(1,0),Fb=(3,0);
draw((-0.6,0)--(5.2,0),gray,Arrow(5));draw((0,-5.2)--(0,5.2),gray,Arrow(5));
real a1=1,a3=3;
path p1=graph(new real(real y){return y^2/(4*a1);},-3.6,3.6,operator ..);
path p3=graph(new real(real y){return y^2/(4*a3);},-4.8,4.8,operator ..);
p1=reflect((0,0),(1,1))*p1;p3=reflect((0,0),(1,1))*p3;
draw(p1,linewidth(0.8));draw(p3,linewidth(0.8));
draw(shift(2,0)*xscale(2)*yscale(4)*unitcircle,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
pair P1=(3,2*sqrt(3)),P3=(1,2*sqrt(3));
dot(Fa);dot(Fb);dot(P1,red);dot(P3,red);
draw(Fa--P1,blue);draw(Fb--P3,blue);
label("$k$",Fa+0.3*(P1-Fa),W,blue);label("$k$",Fb+0.3*(P3-Fb),E,blue);
label("$x$",(5.2,0),S);label("$y$",(0,5.2),W);
label("$a=1$",(3.24,-3.6),E);label("$a=3$",(1.92,-4.8),E);
label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

This is the ellipse with centre $\left(\dfrac {k}{2},0\right)$ and semi-axes $\dfrac {k}{2}$ (horizontal) and $k$ (vertical). Conversely, every point of this ellipse with $0\le x<k$ lies on the parabola with $a=k-x>0$ at distance $k$ from its focus; for instance the origin is the vertex of the parabola with $a=k$. Only the point $(k,0)$ is missed, since it would need $a=0$. So the locus is the ellipse $y^2=4kx-4x^2$ with the single point $(k,0)$ removed. The figure shows $k=4$: on $y^2=4x$ ($a=1$) and $y^2=12x$ ($a=3$) the points at distance $4$ from the focus lie on the dashed ellipse.

A tetrahedron has $4$ vertices, $A,B,C,D$, and every two vertices are joined by an edge (each face is a triangle, and any two vertices lie on a common face).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3.2,-0.6),C=(4.2,0.9),D=(2.0,3.3);
draw(A--B--C,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--C,dashed);
draw(D--A,linewidth(0.9));draw(D--B,linewidth(0.9));draw(D--C,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the edges correspond to the pairs of vertices: $AB,AC,AD,BC,BD,CD$, which is $\dbinom42=6$ edges. Alternatively, it has $4$ triangular faces with $3$ edges each, and each edge is shared by exactly $2$ faces, giving $\dfrac{4\cdot3}{2}=6$. As a check, Euler's formula $V-E+F=4-6+4=2$ holds. The answer is $\boxed{6}$.

Place $B=(0,0)$, $C=(2,0)$, $D=(2,1)$, $A=(0,1)$, and let $X=(x,y)$ with $0<x<2$, $0<y<1$. Triangle $ABX$ has base $AB=1$ on the line $x=0$ and height $x$, so its area is $\dfrac {x}{2}$. Triangle $BCX$ has base $BC=2$ on the line $y=0$ and height $y$, so its area is $y$.

The condition $\dfrac {x}{2}>2y$ means $y<\dfrac {x}{4}$: the point lies below the line through $B$ with slope $\dfrac{1}{4}$, which meets side $CD$ at $\left(2,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),D=(6,3),A=(0,3),Q=(6,1.5);
fill(B--C--Q--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--Q,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$\dfrac{1}{2}$",(C+Q)/2,E);label("$y=\dfrac{x}{4}$",(3.0,1.5),red);
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$2$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The favourable region is the shaded triangle with legs $2$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}$. The rectangle has area $2$, so the probability is $\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the smallest positive integer that can be written as the sum of two different \emph{positive} perfect squares in two different ways?
\item[(b)] What is the answer if $0=0^2$ is also allowed as one of the perfect squares?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The two tangent segments from a point outside a circle to the circle are equal. Let the tangent lengths from $L,M,N,O$ be $\ell,m,n,o$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair T(real a){return dir(a);}
real[] t={95,200,265,330};
pair V(real a,real c){return dir((a+c)/2)/Cos((c-a)/2);}
pair L=V(t[0],t[1]),M=V(t[1],t[2]),Nn=V(t[2],t[3]),O=V(t[3],t[0]+360);
draw(unitcircle,gray);dot((0,0));label("$P$",(0,0),NE);
draw(L--M--Nn--O--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) dot(T(t[i]),blue);
label("$L$",L,dir(L));label("$M$",M,dir(M));label("$N$",Nn,dir(Nn));label("$O$",O,dir(O));
void tl(pair X,real a,string s){pair P=T(a);label(s,(X+P)/2,unit(X+P),blue+fontsize(9pt));}
tl(L,t[0],"$\ell$");tl(L,t[1],"$\ell$");
tl(M,t[1],"$m$");tl(M,t[2],"$m$");
tl(Nn,t[2],"$n$");tl(Nn,t[3],"$n$");
tl(O,t[3],"$o$");tl(O,t[0],"$o$");
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Each side is the sum of the tangent lengths at its ends:
\[
LM=\ell+m,\quad MN=m+n,\quad NO=n+o,\quad OL=o+\ell .
\]
Hence $LM+NO=\ell+m+n+o=MN+OL=13+17=30$, and the perimeter is $30+30=\boxed{60}$.

Let the dimensions be $\ell,w,h$. The surface area gives $2(\ell w+wh+h\ell)=14$, and the space diagonal gives $\ell^2+w^2+h^2=8^2=64$ (Pythagoras twice: the diagonal of the base is $\sqrt{\ell^2+w^2}$, and the space diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs this base diagonal and $h$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=4.2,bb=2.4,c=2.0;pair d=(0.9,0.6);
pair O0=(0,0),X=(a,0),Y=(a,c),Z=(0,c);
draw(O0--X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--X+d--Y+d--Y,linewidth(0.9));draw(Y+d--Z+d--Z,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O0--O0+d,dashed);draw(O0+d--X+d,dashed);draw(O0+d--Z+d,dashed);
draw(O0--Y+d,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$\ell$",(O0+X)/2,S);label("$h$",(X+Y)/2+(0.05,-0.15),E);label("$w$",X+d/2,SE);
label("$8$",(O0+Y+d)/2,NW,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Therefore
\[
(\ell+w+h)^2=\ell^2+w^2+h^2+2(\ell w+wh+h\ell)=64+14=\boxed{78}.
\]
(Such a box exists, for example with $\ell=w\approx0.427$ and $h\approx7.977$; the figure is not to scale.)

Let the side be $s$ and the radius $r$. The centre $O$ lies on the perpendicular to $EN$ at $M$ (the radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent), so $O$ is on the horizontal line through $M$ at distance $r$ from $EN$. This line also passes through the midpoint $H$ of $ZG$, and $OH=s-r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Z=(0,0),Ep=(16,0),Nn=(16,16),G=(0,16),M=(16,8),O=(6,8),H=(0,8);
draw(Z--Ep--Nn--G--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,10),blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(O);dot(M);
draw(O--M,dashed);draw(O--Z,red+dashed);draw(O--H,dashed);
draw((0,8)--(0.9,8)--(0.9,8.9)--(0,8.9),linewidth(0.5));
draw((16,8)--(15.1,8)--(15.1,8.9)--(16,8.9),linewidth(0.5));
label("$Z$",Z,SW);label("$E$",Ep,SE);label("$N$",Nn,NE);label("$G$",G,NW);
label("$M$",M,E);label("$O$",O,N);label("$H$",H,W);
label("$r$",(O+M)/2,N);label("$r$",(O+Z)/2,SE,red);
label("$s-r$",(O+H)/2,S);label("$\dfrac {s}{2}$",(Z+H)/2,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In right triangle $OHZ$, $OZ=r$, $OH=s-r$ and $HZ=\dfrac {s}{2}$, so
\[
r^2=(s-r)^2+\dfrac{s^2}{4}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 0=s^2-2sr+\dfrac{s^2}{4}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{5s}{8}.
\]
With $r=10$ we get $s=16$, and the area is $16^2=\boxed{256}$.

A quadrilateral with an inscribed circle has equal sums of opposite sides (equal tangent segments). So the two legs together equal $4+8=12$, and each leg is $6$.

Drop a perpendicular from an end of the short base to the long base. It cuts off a segment of length $\dfrac{8-4}{2}=2$, so the height is $\sqrt{6^2-2^2}=\sqrt{32}=4\sqrt2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=4*sqrt(2),r=2*sqrt(2);
pair A=(-4,0),B=(4,0),C=(2,h),D=(-2,h),Q=(2,0),O=(0,r);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,r),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--Q,dashed);draw(Q--Q+(-0.35,0)--Q+(-0.35,0.35)--Q+(0,0.35),linewidth(0.5));
label("$4$",(D+C)/2,N);draw((-4,-1.2)--(4,-1.2),Arrows(4));label("$8$",(0,-1.2),S);label("$6$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("$2$",(Q+B)/2,S);label("$4\sqrt2$",(C+Q)/2+(0,-0.6),W);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The circle touches both bases, which are parallel, so its diameter equals the distance between them, the height. The diameter is $\boxed{4\sqrt2}$.

From $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}a^2=25\sqrt3$ the side of the triangle is $a=10$.

Let the square $PQRT$ have side $s$, with $P,Q$ on $AB$, $R$ on $BC$ and $T$ on $AC$. By symmetry $AP=QB=x$ with $2x+s=10$. In the right triangle $QBR$, the angle at $B$ is $60^\circ$ and $QR=s$, so $s=x\tan60^\circ=x\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=10,s=20*sqrt(3)-30;
pair A=(0,0),B=(a,0),C=(a/2,a*sqrt(3)/2);
pair P=((a-s)/2,0),Q=((a+s)/2,0),R=((a+s)/2,s),T=((a-s)/2,s);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
