Then $K=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},1\right)$, $L=\left(0,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $M=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$, $N=\left(1,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$. Line $AK$ is $y=2x$ and line $BL$ is $y=\dfrac{1-x}{2}$; they meet at $\left(\dfrac{1}{5},\dfrac{2}{5}\right)$. The figure is unchanged by a quarter turn about the centre $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$ (which maps $A\to B\to C\to D\to A$ and $K\to L\to M\to N\to K$), so the four corners of the small quadrilateral are the images of this point:
\[
\left(\dfrac{1}{5},\dfrac{2}{5}\right),\ \left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{1}{5}\right),\ \left(\dfrac{4}{5},\dfrac{3}{5}\right),\ \left(\dfrac{2}{5},\dfrac{4}{5}\right).
\]
It is a square with side $\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{2}{5}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{1}{5}\right)^2}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}$, so its area is $\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the unit square. Hence the ratio is $\dfrac{1}{5}$ for every parallelogram. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Here $K=(1,3)$, $L=(0,2)$, $M=(1,0)$, $N=(2,1)$, and $ABCD$ (a right trapezoid) has area $\dfrac{(4+2)\cdot2}{2}=6$. Intersecting consecutive segments gives the corners
\[
AK\cap BL=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad BL\cap CM=\left(\dfrac{4}{3},\dfrac{2}{3}\right),\quad CM\cap DN=\left(\dfrac{12}{7},\dfrac{10}{7}\right),\quad DN\cap AK=\left(\dfrac{8}{9},\dfrac{8}{3}\right).
\]
By the shoelace formula the small quadrilateral has area $\dfrac{151}{126}$, so the ratio is
\[
\dfrac{151}{126\cdot6}=\boxed{\dfrac{151}{756}}\ne\dfrac{1}{5}=\dfrac{151.2}{756} .
\]
So the claim ``always $\dfrac{1}{5}$'' is false for general quadrilaterals; it holds for parallelograms.

question:
A student claims: ``To solve a system of equations in $n$ variables uniquely, you need at least $n$ equations.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false for general (nonlinear) equations by giving a single equation in two real variables with exactly one solution.
\item[(b)] Prove that the claim is true for \emph{linear} equations: if a system of $m$ linear equations in $n$ real variables has $m<n$, then it has either no solution or infinitely many.
\end{enumerate}

Regular polygons with $5$, $5$ and $12$ sides do not fit around a point, but two regular pentagons and a regular decagon do.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Check both statements.
\item[(b)] Prove that there is no edge-to-edge tiling of the plane by regular polygons in which every vertex is surrounded by two regular pentagons and one regular decagon.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A regular $n$-gon has interior angles $\dfrac{(n-2)180^\circ}{n}$: $108^\circ$ for $n=5$, $144^\circ$ for $n=10$ and $150^\circ$ for $n=12$. Since $108^\circ+108^\circ+150^\circ=366^\circ\ne360^\circ$, two pentagons and a dodecagon do not fit around a point, while $108^\circ+108^\circ+144^\circ=360^\circ$, so two pentagons and a decagon do.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair V=(0,0);
path poly(pair P,real d,int n){pair[] q;q.push(P);real t=d;pair X=P;for(int i=1;i<n;++i){X=X+dir(t);q.push(X);t+=360/n;}path g=q[0];for(int i=1;i<n;++i) g=g--q[i];return g--cycle;}
path p1=poly(V,0,5),p2=poly(V,108,5),d1=poly(V,216,10);
fill(p1,palegreen);fill(p2,palegreen);fill(d1,paleblue);
draw(p1,linewidth(0.9));draw(p2,linewidth(0.9));draw(d1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(V,0.18,0,108));draw(arc(V,0.18,108,216));draw(arc(V,0.18,216,360));
label("$108^\circ$",0.5*dir(54),fontsize(7pt));label("$108^\circ$",0.5*dir(162),fontsize(7pt));label("$144^\circ$",0.42*dir(288),fontsize(7pt));
label("pentagon",0.95*dir(54),fontsize(7pt));label("pentagon",0.95*dir(162),fontsize(7pt));label("decagon",1.618*dir(288),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Suppose such a tiling exists, and look at one pentagon $P$. At each vertex $V$ of $P$ there are exactly three tiles: $P$, one other pentagon and one decagon. The two edges of $P$ at $V$ separate $P$ from its two neighbours at $V$; each of these edges is shared with one of the other two tiles at $V$ (the tiling is edge-to-edge). So at every vertex of $P$, the two edges of $P$ meeting there are shared with tiles of \emph{different} kinds: one with a pentagon and one with the decagon.

Going around $P$, the kinds of the neighbours across its edges $e_1,e_2,e_3,e_4,e_5$ must therefore alternate: pentagon, decagon, pentagon, decagon, \dots\ But there are $5$ edges, an odd number, so $e_5$ and $e_1$, which meet at a vertex of $P$, would have neighbours of the same kind. This contradiction shows that no such tiling exists. $\blacksquare$

(The same argument shows that a vertex type $a.b.c$ with $a$ odd can occur everywhere in a tiling only if $b=c$; vertex types that fit around one point need not extend to a tiling.)

question:
The number $145$ equals the sum of the factorials of its digits: $145=1!+4!+5!$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Verify that $40585$ has the same property.
\item[(b)] Prove that every positive integer with this property has at most $7$ digits.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Solve $\sqrt{2x+7}=x+2$ over the real numbers. Squaring both sides gives a quadratic with two roots; show that only one of them solves the original equation, and explain why.
\item[(b)] Explain why cubing both sides of an equation never introduces such extra solutions.
\end{enumerate}

Let $x>0$. We write $x$ as a sum of any number of positive real numbers and multiply those numbers together.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that if exactly $k$ numbers are used, the largest possible product is $\left(\dfrac {x}{k}\right)^k$.
\item[(b)] Show that the largest possible product overall is $\left(\dfrac {x}{k}\right)^k$ for $k=\left\lfloor \dfrac {x}{e}\right\rfloor$ or $k=\left\lceil\dfrac {x}{e}\right\rceil$ (whichever gives the larger value, with $k\ge1$).
\item[(c)] Find the largest possible product when $x=10$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The $k$-th term is $k^{e_k}$ with $e_k=(k+1)^{k+2}$. The units digit of $k^e$ ($e\ge1$) depends only on the units digit of $k$ and on $e$ modulo $4$ (the units digits of powers repeat with period dividing $4$).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{$k$ odd.} Then $k+1$ is even and $k+2\ge3$, so $8\mid e_k$ and $e_k\equiv0\pmod4$. The units digit of $k^{e_k}$ is that of $k^4$: $1,1,5,1,1$ for $k$ ending in $1,3,5,7,9$.
\item \textbf{$k$ even.} Then $k+1$ is odd and $k+2$ is even, so $e_k$ is an odd square and $e_k\equiv1\pmod4$. The units digit of $k^{e_k}$ is that of $k$ itself: $2,4,6,8,0$.
\end{itemize}
So every block of ten consecutive values of $k$ contributes units digits with sum $1+1+5+1+1+2+4+6+8+0=29$. The terms $k=1,\dots,2010$ form $201$ blocks, and $k=2011,\dots,2018$ contribute $1+1+5+1+2+4+6+8=28$. The units digit of the sum is that of $201\cdot29+28=5857$, namely $\boxed{7}$.

Let $x=[DXA]$. Triangles $AXB$ and $CXB$ have bases $XA$ and $XC$ on line $AC$ and the same apex $B$, so they have the same height and
\[
\dfrac{[AXB]}{[CXB]}=\dfrac{XA}{XC}.
\]
In the same way, triangles $AXD$ and $CXD$ share the apex $D$, so $\dfrac{[AXD]}{[CXD]}=\dfrac{XA}{XC}$. Therefore
\[
\dfrac{x}{5}=\dfrac{8}{15}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=\boxed{\dfrac{8}{3}}.
\]
Equivalently, $[AXB]\cdot[CXD]=[BXC]\cdot[DXA]$.

