\textbf{Locating $D$.} In the cyclic quadrilateral $CDFE$ the exterior angle at $D$ equals the interior angle at $E$: $\angle ADB=\angle ADF=\angle FEC=\angle AEC$. From triangles $ABD$ and $AEC$,
\[
180^\circ-\alpha-\angle ABD=180^\circ-\dfrac{\alpha}{2}-\gamma\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\angle ABD=\gamma-\dfrac{\alpha}{2} .
\]
Here $\tan\dfrac{\alpha}{2}=\dfrac{\sin\alpha}{1+\cos\alpha}=\dfrac{\ \dfrac{4}{5}\ }{\ \dfrac{8}{5}\ }=\dfrac{1}{2}$, so
\[
\tan\angle ABD=\dfrac{\dfrac{3}{4}-\dfrac{1}{2}}{1+\dfrac{3}{8}}=\dfrac{2}{11}.
\]
Since $\angle ABD$ is measured from $BA$ (the $y$-axis), line $BD$ is $x=\dfrac{2}{11}y$. It meets $AC$: $\dfrac {x}{4}+\dfrac {y}{3}=1$ at $D=\left(\dfrac{12}{25},\dfrac{66}{25}\right)$, and $AE$: $2x+y=3$ at $F=\left(\dfrac{2}{5},\dfrac{11}{5}\right)$.

\textbf{The centre.} $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=\dfrac{11}{4}$ of $CE$; write $O=\left(\dfrac{11}{4},k\right)$. From $OC^2=OD^2$:
