label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$4$",(1,s),NW);label("$2$",(5.5,s/2),E);label("$2$",(2.5,2.5s),W);label("$4$",(4,2s),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Diagonals.} In the right triangle $ADC$, $AC^2=AD^2+DC^2=16+12=28$. In triangle $ABD$, by the law of cosines, $BD^2=36+16-2\cdot6\cdot4\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=28$. So
\[
AC=BD=\boxed{2\sqrt7}.
\]

\textbf{Area.} $[ABCD]=[ABP]-[PDC]=9\sqrt3-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\cdot2\sqrt3=\boxed{7\sqrt3}$.

Let $D$ and $E$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A$ and $B$, on $BC$ and $CA$. In quadrilateral $CEHD$ the angles at $D$ and $E$ are right angles, so $\angle DHE=360^\circ-90^\circ-90^\circ-\angle C=180^\circ-\angle C$. The angles $\angle AHB$ and $\angle DHE$ are vertical angles (the figure marks $\angle DHE$), so
\[
126^\circ=\angle AHB=180^\circ-\angle C\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \angle C=54^\circ,
\]
and $\angle B=180^\circ-80^\circ-54^\circ=46^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0);
pair C=extension(A,A+dir(80),B,B+dir(134));
pair D=A+dot(C-B,A-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);
pair Dd=B+dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);
pair Ee=A+dot(B-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);
pair H=extension(A,Dd,B,Ee);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dd,blue);draw(B--Ee,blue);
real q=0.3;
pair u=unit(A-Dd),v=unit(C-Dd);draw(Dd+q*u--Dd+q*(u+v)--Dd+q*v);
u=unit(B-Ee);v=unit(C-Ee);draw(Ee+q*u--Ee+q*(u+v)--Ee+q*v);
draw(arc(H,0.55,degrees(Dd-H),degrees(Ee-H)),red);
label("$126^\circ$",H+0.95*dir(75),red);
draw(arc(A,0.65,0,80));label("$80^\circ$",A+1.15*dir(18));
dot(H);dot(Dd);dot(Ee);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$H$",H,N);label("$D$",Dd,NE);label("$E$",Ee,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In a triangle the longer side is opposite the larger angle. The angles are $\angle A=80^\circ>\angle C=54^\circ>\angle B=46^\circ$, so the \textbf{longest side is $BC$} (opposite $A$) and the \textbf{shortest side is $CA$} (opposite $B$): $\boxed{CA<AB<BC}$.

question:
Cards numbered $1$ to $100$ are laid out in order. First Faris takes every card whose number is a multiple of $7$, and after that every remaining card whose number contains the digit $7$. Then, from the remaining cards, Lamija takes every card whose number is divisible by $5$, and after that every remaining card whose number contains the digit $5$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Who gets more cards, and by how many?
\item[(b)] Answer the same question if instead Lamija goes first with her ``$5$ rule'' and Faris then applies his ``$7$ rule'' to the remaining cards.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The sides of the first two squares are $\dfrac{40}{4}=10$ m and $\dfrac{96}{4}=24$ m, so their areas are $100\text{ m}^2$ and $576\text{ m}^2$. The third square has area $100+576=676\text{ m}^2$, so its side is $\sqrt{676}=26$ m.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((13,0)--(37,0)--(37,24)--(13,24)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((40,0)--(66,0)--(66,26)--(40,26)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
label("$10$",(5,0),S);label("$24$",(25,0),S);label("$26$",(53,0),S);
label("$100$",(5,5));label("$576$",(25,12));label("$676$",(53,13),blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its perimeter is $4\cdot26=\boxed{104\text{ m}}$. (Note that $10^2+24^2=26^2$: the three sides form a right triangle.)

Put the centre $O$ of $k$ at the origin and let $ABCD$ have side $2$, with $AB$ on the line $y=1$; then $k$ has radius $\sqrt2$. By symmetry the small square is symmetric about the $y$-axis (the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ is the axis of symmetry of the figure, and the chord $GH$ of $k$ parallel to $AB$ has the same perpendicular bisector). If its side is $s$, then $G=\left(\dfrac {s}{2},\ 1+s\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=sqrt(2);
pair O=(0,0),A=(-1,1),B=(1,1),C=(1,-1),D=(-1,-1);
pair Ep=(-0.2,1),F=(0.2,1),G=(0.2,1.4),H=(-0.2,1.4);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ep--F--G--H--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--G,red+dashed);draw(O--(0,1),gray+dashed);
